Google cloud next 2017 has hosted a  plethora of talks detailing the future  of cloud if you didn't get a chance to  check out meteo at Mel's talk running  net on Google cloud then stay tuned  because here's the recap historically  dotnet and Google cloud platform haven't  exactly played well together the reasons  for this are complex but one of the  biggest challenges has been around the  language and tooling not working very  well with scalable Linux VMs but brace  yourselves that's no longer the case  improved Linux support for the dotnet  environment has allowed the framework to  extend its reach and the availability of  Windows VMs on Google compute engine  opens doors for new types of  applications to run on Google  infrastructure see the decision to run  net on GCP is no longer an issue of  features and supports it's about  choosing the best cloud platform for  your needs and when it comes to dotnet  development GCP has some new features to  help you be more productive let's start  by looking at the libraries and tools  we've provided a suite of runtime  libraries that make it easy for your  application to interface with the full  suite of GCP services that includes  everything from the vision API and  natural speech API authentication and  data store all available for your dotnet  app we're also providing visual studio  plugins and powershell commandlets to  help you manage your DCP resources from  within the tools you're already using on  a day to day basis on the compute side  of things we've got support to boot a VM  with Windows on it but we also go  further by offering Google cloud  launcher which gives you one-click  deployment to Windows servers that come  pre-installed with things like asp.net  and the Google cloud platform dotnet  libraries but if you've already done  that legwork yourself and are running  say a container with Windows and asp.net  core on it don't fret you can deploy  that with ease using App Engine flex  environment or our kubernetes platform  Google container engine and the best  part is that all of this is running on  Google's infrastructure which already  offers fast vm provisioning simple auto  scaling and per minute billing  not to mention access to manage services  like a BigTable datastore dataflow cloud  storage and genomics processing  GCP also provides the Federation of  tools and services which make your  deployments production-ready the health  check system can monitor your instances  and a fully expanded and accessible  orphaned IM system allows you to control  who and what has access to everything  plus there's integration to stackdriver  which gives you logging reporting  tracing and monitoring of your cloud  service right out of the box  check out the recording of met a full  session to see a bunch of cool demos of  asp.net running on google cloud and if  you want more recaps on great next  content make sure to check out the rest  of the playlist and don't forget the  next world tour which is coming soon to  a city near you  [Music]
 

